# Every source archive the bundle is built from, and where to get it.
#
# The build itself downloads nothing. fetch-sources.sh reads this file on the host,
# fills the sources/ directory and records a checksum for each archive in sha256sums;
# build-in-container.sh then resolves every package out of that directory by name and
# refuses to build if an archive is missing or its checksum does not match. That split
# is what makes a build reproducible from a known set of bytes rather than from
# whatever the upstream mirrors happen to be serving at the time, and it is why a
# compromised or hijacked mirror cannot reach a release unnoticed.
#
#   <local filename>|<url> [fallback url...]
#
# The local filename is this project's own choice and is what both scripts agree on,
# so it does not have to match the last path component of the URL -- GitHub's
# /archive/ URLs end in a bare tag like "v1.1.0.tar.gz", which says nothing about
# what is inside. ${VAR} references are expanded against versions.env. A fallback is
# tried only when the one before it fails, and is expected to serve the same bytes;
# an upstream that cannot is a reason to drop it, not to add it.

zlib-${ZLIB_VERSION}.tar.gz|https://zlib.net/fossils/zlib-${ZLIB_VERSION}.tar.gz https://github.com/madler/zlib/releases/download/v${ZLIB_VERSION}/zlib-${ZLIB_VERSION}.tar.gz
